\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-78682" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cd3c3ab57d3f4eee40fa133cc881384f001d3a25c4d860095ddb8674be6cfd87"} {"question" "q-78682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-78682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-78682" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/e5bf161de62bc1b38aaeb87a327df551e8a7043f2867caa060d652cd065bece9.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/6efc63426eae95430b0b5ddee44ab1523f69eb00ba26720374f7246712f7088a.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse in the **fourth quadrant** (where $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\le 0$).\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\np=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cd3c3ab57d3f4eee40fa133cc881384f001d3a25c4d860095ddb8674be6cfd87"} {"question" "q-327831" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327831" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327831" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327831" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/ab9ef90cf863fee6776667a967d5939ba3564564d5d7626e07aaa868baea6a87.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,1),$ with $a=4$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we again interpret $\\theta $ as the central angle measured from the horizontal line through the center $(h,k).$ On the quarter-arc where both $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\ge 0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\ge 0$ (moving from the rightmost point to the topmost point), the parameter must satisfy $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/df6c12657a6a487f93ec3a6213be8ea16e68d363001bdf0ddb8ff68b5201a8c4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cd3c3ab57d3f4eee40fa133cc881384f001d3a25c4d860095ddb8674be6cfd87"} {"question" "q-327847" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327847" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327847" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327847" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/8a04ad6bb8ce108e4f63fddcd5c178ec855a646a14761ad989a9e6c9328ddbd0.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/6db43ff41344f292c8cb5805febd175088fe068fe89927bade0c6fc2246c8781.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cd3c3ab57d3f4eee40fa133cc881384f001d3a25c4d860095ddb8674be6cfd87"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3 \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5 \\\\ \\frac{y+5}{2\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327798" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cd3c3ab57d3f4eee40fa133cc881384f001d3a25c4d860095ddb8674be6cfd87"} {"question" "q-327798" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-327798" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{(x-3)^2}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^2}{28}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-15843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-15843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-15843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cd3c3ab57d3f4eee40fa133cc881384f001d3a25c4d860095ddb8674be6cfd87"} {"question" "q-15843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-15843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-15843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-15792" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-15792" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-15792" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cd3c3ab57d3f4eee40fa133cc881384f001d3a25c4d860095ddb8674be6cfd87"} {"question" "q-15792" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-15792" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-15792" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-106408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-106408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(x-2) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{(x-2)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3 \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2(y-3) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{(y-3)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-2)}^{2}+4{(y-3)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n4{(x-2)}^{2}+4{(y-3)}^{2}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-106408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cd3c3ab57d3f4eee40fa133cc881384f001d3a25c4d860095ddb8674be6cfd87"} {"question" "q-106408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-106408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4{(x-2)}^{2}+4{(y-3)}^{2}=1"] ["math" "4{(x-2)}^{2}-4{(y-3)}^{2}=1"] ["math" "4{(x-3)}^{2}+4{(y-2)}^{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}{(x-2)}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{(y-3)}^{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}{(x-2)}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{(y-3)}^{2}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-106408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{(x-2)}^{2}+4{(y-3)}^{2}=1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-106407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-106407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-106407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cd3c3ab57d3f4eee40fa133cc881384f001d3a25c4d860095ddb8674be6cfd87"} {"question" "q-106407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-106407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{4}{9}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{9}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}{y}^{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "q-106407" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{y}^{2}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "e525b3ca26f6e25cbdc38019b7cd082199eb5ee69e6ccad16653e953af3a83bd"} {"question" "e-6814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6814.json" "file_sha256" "c051186d7c8fff3106f511a71105516c018c45a15663b739b60b2d61eeeb1006"} {"question" "e-6814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6814.json" "file_sha256" "c051186d7c8fff3106f511a71105516c018c45a15663b739b60b2d61eeeb1006"} {"question" "e-6816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6816.json" "file_sha256" "9b315a82feab1886a14fe30608a7c61e76937596abfcbbca7cd0b54946ad9aca"} {"question" "e-6816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6816.json" "file_sha256" "9b315a82feab1886a14fe30608a7c61e76937596abfcbbca7cd0b54946ad9aca"} {"question" "e-6818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6818.json" "file_sha256" "ad968429e0e578f1412935b842cb4ebbe7c9dd2bc2e5e7fdfd70aba9eff81b3c"} {"question" "e-6818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0, \\pi\\right],$ we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6818.json" "file_sha256" "ad968429e0e578f1412935b842cb4ebbe7c9dd2bc2e5e7fdfd70aba9eff81b3c"} {"question" "e-6815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6815.json" "file_sha256" "81c656737095311c331acafe47fb3ed4ebe4167da8c2eb001f4e8ed6786cc297"} {"question" "e-6815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6815.json" "file_sha256" "81c656737095311c331acafe47fb3ed4ebe4167da8c2eb001f4e8ed6786cc297"}] "usage" {"e-6816" [] "e-6818" [] "q-78682" [] "e-6814" [] "e-6815" []} "basis" 1106 "retractions" [[17592186152718 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/q-78682.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 3\\cos \\theta , y = 2\\sin \\theta $.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta $?"] [17592186151916 :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1$, as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/e4dfe9db8587b4dfa4fae9c4610106a3.png)"] [17592186151916 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n$x= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0)$.\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1$. Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n$x= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$"] [17592186151918 :question/problem "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4$, as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/ad11664b2d980183e25e80a1d5c231cd.png)"] [17592186151918 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$x= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k)$.\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2)$, $a=6$ and $b=4$. Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n$x=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).$"] [17592186151919 :question/problem "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/83eed01dabcfcec70e64963213c46397.png)"] [17592186151919 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n$x= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4$. Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n$x= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2746/Source/Images/86476fd8ded16a035b000e1865623698.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to\n\n$$\n\\left[0, \\pi\\right]\n$$\n\nwe get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right]\n$$"] [17592186151917 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n$x= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$."] [17592186151917 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta$. We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 4\\cos \\theta  \\\\\n\\frac{x}{4} &= \\cos \\theta  \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4^{2}} &= \\cos^{2} \\theta  \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{16} &= \\cos^{2} \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}y + 1 & = 2 \\sin   \\theta \\\\ \\frac{y + 1}{2} & = \\sin   \\theta \\\\ \\frac{( y + 1 {)}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & = {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta \\\\ \\frac{( y + 1 {)}^{2}}{4} & = {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  .\\end{array}$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} + \\frac{( y + 1 {)}^{2}}{4} & = {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta  + {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta  = 1 .\\end{array}$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186151219 17592186151220 17592186151221 17592186151222 17592186151223] "immutable_fields" [[17592186151218 :answer-field/correct] [17592186151218 :answer-field/id] [17592186151218 :answer-field/key] [17592186151218 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
